1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
Alright, the next section deals with the colon.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,060
The first disorder that we want to talk about

3
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:05,380
is Hirschsprung's disease.

4
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:07,820
In this disorder, you have defective relaxation

5
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:09,880
and peristalsis of the rectum

6
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:11,800
and the distal sigmoid colon.

7
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:13,720
There's an association of Hirschsprung's disease

8
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,000
with Down syndrome.

9
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,700
That's a particularly high yield thing to be aware of.

10
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:20,120
Now, the disorder arises because there's a congenital

11
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:22,280
failure of the ganglion cells to descend.

12
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:24,080
Let me remind you that the ganglion cells

13
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,400
are derived from the neural crest

14
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:30,000
and that they descend into the wall of the bowel

15
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,760
and when they're present in the wall of the bowel

16
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:33,240
they have particular function.

17
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:35,040
But before you can understand that

18
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:37,420
and how it relates to the disease,

19
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:38,840
you've got to understand normal.

20
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:40,180
So let's go back and remind ourselves

21
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:43,220
that the bowel wall is comprised of mucosa,

22
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:47,560
a submucosa, a muscularis propria, and a serosa.

23
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:49,340
And then remember that the muscularis propria

24
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:51,060
has an inner circular layer

25
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:53,120
and an outer longitudinal layer.

26
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:56,020
Now, present within the wall of this bowel

27
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:57,640
is also ganglion cells

28
00:00:57,640 --> 00:00:59,740
and you've got two groups of ganglion cells.

29
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:01,300
You've got one set here

30
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:04,180
which is called Auerbach's myenteric plexus.

31
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:06,720
This is present between the layers of the lamina propria

32
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:09,160
and most important is that it's important for

33
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:11,080
motility and relaxation.

34
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:15,000
So you need these ganglion cells to have good peristalsis

35
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,860
and also to be able to relax properly.

36
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:20,280
So that's the first group of ganglion cells.

37
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:21,580
The second set of ganglion cells

38
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:23,700
is actually present in the submucosa.

39
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:26,460
It's called Meisner's submucosal plexus

40
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:31,900
and this helps to regulate blood flow, secretions, absorption, etc.

41
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:35,440
And so you need both of these sets of ganglion cells for proper function.

42
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:39,900
Now what happens again in Hirschsprung's disease is that in the rectum

43
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:44,420
and possibly the distal sigmoid colon, but let's focus on the rectum,

44
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:48,120
you are going to be lacking the ganglion cells of both layers.

45
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:51,100
And so if the ganglion cells of both layers are lacking,

46
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:55,880
you're not going to have good peristalsis so you won't be able to pass stool through appropriately

47
00:01:55,880 --> 00:02:00,100
and you won't have good relaxation so you won't be able to relax the rectum appropriately

48
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:03,560
to get the stool to move forward and eventually out of the body.

49
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:07,560
And so the clinical features of this disorder are all going to be based on that central theme

50
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:09,500
which is that there's going to be obstruction.

51
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:15,600
So the babies would fail to pass meconium, there'd be an empty vault on digital rectal exam,

52
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:19,340
there'd be dilation of the bowel proximal to the area of obstruction.

53
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:24,680
So imagine if this is the rectum here and you can't relax it properly and you don't have good peristalsis,

54
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:30,980
then behind it you're going to get massive dilatation of the bowel that again is proximal to that area.

55
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:36,660
And there's a risk here, of course, as you get dilation and further dilation, there's a risk of rupture.

56
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:41,600
The way by which the diagnosis is made in this particular disorder is rectal suction biopsy

57
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:44,960
and the rectal suction biopsy will show a lack of ganglion cells.

58
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:49,840
Now it's important to note that you need to do rectal suction biopsy and that's particularly high yield.

59
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:54,080
And the idea here is that the standard biopsy simply only takes a piece of mucosa,

60
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:58,880
but if you take a piece of mucosa you won't be able to ask the question of whether the ganglion cells are absent or present

61
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:03,500
because the mucosa normally does not have ganglion cells to begin with.

62
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:09,000
So basically what you need to do is you need to actually create a little bit of suction against the rectal wall

63
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,440
so that you actually pull the submucosa into the biopsy.

64
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:16,920
And by looking at the submucosa you can ask the question, are the ganglion cells present or absent?

65
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:21,820
Because they should normally be present there and if they're missing then you can truly say that they are absent.

66
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:26,360
So very important and very high yield to know that you've got to use the rectal suction biopsy

67
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:29,420
to be able to truly look at the ganglion cells.

68
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:34,920
The treatment of this disorder is to resect the area of involved bowel, so for example if you're looking at the disease

69
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:39,920
and this is the rectum, you may have no ganglion cells here and then no ganglion cells here

70
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:45,300
and then no ganglion cells here, but eventually you'll come to a region where there are ganglion cells.

71
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:50,300
And so what the surgeon will do is resect this area that does not have ganglion cells

72
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:53,780
and leave behind the area that does have ganglion cells.

73
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:59,280
Okay, the next disorder is colonic diverticula. The idea here is that you get a diverticulum or an outpouching

74
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:02,280
and of course in this particular case it would involve the colon.

75
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:10,280
The outpouching is a pseudodiverticulum. Remember that the pseudodiverticulum means that you only push out part of the wall instead of the entire wall.

76
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:14,280
So that's what the pseudo refers to, part as opposed to the entire wall.

77
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:20,280
And in this particular case it happens to be the mucosa and the submucosa that go through the muscularis propria.

78
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:27,780
So the idea here is that if you've got your mucosa and then you've got submucosa and then you've got muscularis propria

79
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:38,780
and of course then you've got serosa, what happens is that you get an outpouching of both the submucosa and the mucosa like this through the muscularis propria.

80
00:04:38,780 --> 00:04:40,780
And then of course this would become normal again.

81
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:44,780
Now the reason why patients get these is because it's related to wall stress.

82
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:52,280
Anything that's going to increase wall stress is going to increase the risk of pushing the submucosa and the mucosa through the muscularis propria.

83
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:59,280
So it's associated with constipation, a low fiber diet, which again both of these would result in straining commonly seen in older adults.

84
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:05,280
The more tension on the wall over time, the increased risk of developing colonic diverticula.

85
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:12,780
What's important to note is that it arises where the vasa recta traverse the muscularis propria and that's a weak point in the colon wall.

86
00:05:12,780 --> 00:05:15,780
And the most common location is the sigmoid colon.

87
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:21,780
The idea here again being that if I were to draw you this image again, so this was the mucosa.

88
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:25,780
And then again here this is the submucosa.

89
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:31,280
And then you've got the muscularis propria, which I'm going to draw particularly thick here.

90
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,280
So this is the muscularis propria.

91
00:05:33,280 --> 00:05:41,280
What happens is remember that you've got these veins called the vasa recta and the vasa recta, they traverse the muscularis propria when they come up.

92
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:46,280
And as they traverse the muscularis propria, they are then going to weaken the wall slightly at that point,

93
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:50,280
which then creates the opportunity for this diverticulum to form.

94
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:58,780
Now what's very important is that notice that this diverticulum is right next to this vein and that's going to be very important when I talk about some of the complications in a moment.

95
00:05:58,780 --> 00:06:00,780
Here's a picture of a diverticulum.

96
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:06,780
It's very similar to what I've drawn except that you're looking at the surface of the colon and this hole represents the diverticulum.

97
00:06:06,780 --> 00:06:09,780
Here's a histologic image highlighting what I've already drawn for you.

98
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:15,780
Of course you've got your mucosa and your submucosa as I'm writing up here in the upper left hand side of the screen.

99
00:06:15,780 --> 00:06:24,780
And then this is the muscularis propria and you can clearly see that both the mucosa and the submucosa have come through the muscularis propria.

100
00:06:24,780 --> 00:06:31,780
Now of course that would arise in the region where the vein is actually coming up, which again is a weak point in the wall.

101
00:06:31,780 --> 00:06:40,780
Now diverticuli are usually asymptomatic and many patients develop these as they age, but there are some associated complications to be aware of in their high yield.

102
00:06:40,780 --> 00:06:48,780
The first is rectal bleeding. You recall that the diverticulum is right up against the wall of the vein, so it wouldn't be hard for bleeding to occur.

103
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:56,780
And these patients, when they get bleeding, they get something called hematochesia where they get bright red blood being passed into the toilet, for example.

104
00:06:56,780 --> 00:07:01,780
You can also get diverticulitis. That's easy to understand if this is the diverticulum.

105
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:09,780
If you were to obstruct this diverticulum with fecal material, that would give you inflammation of the wall eventually, which would be diverticulitis.

106
00:07:09,780 --> 00:07:14,780
And that is going to present with what they call left-sided appendicitis-like symptoms.

107
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:19,780
So it's sort of like appendicitis, but of course appendicitis would be on the right side, this would be on the left side.

108
00:07:19,780 --> 00:07:25,780
And then another important complication could be a fistula. Remember that a fistula is a connection between two tubes.

109
00:07:25,780 --> 00:07:33,780
So in this particular case, imagine that you've got this diverticulum here. Imagine that the wall gets inflamed, and when the wall gets inflamed, that weakens the wall,

110
00:07:33,780 --> 00:07:42,780
and then the wall subsequently bursts or ruptures, and then it plugs into another tube. For example, in this case, let's make this the bladder.

111
00:07:42,780 --> 00:07:50,780
So the classic type of fistula that you often can see on an examination would be something called a colovesicular fistula,

112
00:07:50,780 --> 00:07:55,780
where you get a diverticulum that is plugged into, for example, the bladder.

113
00:07:55,780 --> 00:08:00,780
Now the way by which this would present is that you would have air or stool in the urine.

114
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:06,780
The next disorder is angiodysplasia. Angio means blood vessel. Dysplasia means that the blood vessel has become malformed.

115
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:13,780
So it's an acquired malformation of the mucosal and submucosal vessels.

116
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:20,780
What is important to note is that it usually arises in the cecum, in the right colon, due to high wall tension.

117
00:08:20,780 --> 00:08:24,780
And this is going to present as hematochesia in an older adult. I'll make this very simple for you.

118
00:08:24,780 --> 00:08:31,780
If you have high stress in the left colon, you're going to get diverticula, and those can present with hematochesia.

119
00:08:31,780 --> 00:08:39,780
If you have high stress in the right side of the colon, you get angiodysplasia, and that can also present as hematochesia.

120
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:44,780
So that's the way I remember it. High stress on the left and high stress on the right can result in hematochesia,

121
00:08:44,780 --> 00:08:47,780
but the pathogenesis is a little bit different.

122
00:08:47,780 --> 00:08:53,780
Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia. This is a hereditary disorder in which patients have telangiectasias.

123
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:59,780
And telangiectasias are dilated, thin-walled blood vessels. Now this is autosomal dominant.

124
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:03,780
It particularly occurs in the nasopharynx and the GI tract.

125
00:09:03,780 --> 00:09:07,780
And when these rupture, when these telangiectasias rupture, the patient presents with bleeding.

126
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:12,780
Here's a classic picture of a patient with hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia.

127
00:09:12,780 --> 00:09:17,780
And these little red areas here represent the telangiectatic blood vessels.

128
00:09:17,780 --> 00:09:22,780
Again, anywhere from the oropharynx slash nasopharynx through the GI tract.

129
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:25,780
So it's a cause of bleeding in the GI tract.

130
00:09:25,780 --> 00:09:30,780
The next disorder is ischemic colitis. This is where you get ischemic damage to the colon.

131
00:09:30,780 --> 00:09:36,780
Usually it occurs at the splenic flexure, and the most common cause is atherosclerosis of the SMA.

132
00:09:36,780 --> 00:09:42,780
Now, if you get atherosclerosis of the SMA, let's pretend that this is the SMA and you've got atherosclerosis,

133
00:09:42,780 --> 00:09:44,780
now you're going to be decreasing the blood flow.

134
00:09:44,780 --> 00:09:48,780
And when you decrease the blood flow, the area that's furthest away,

135
00:09:48,780 --> 00:09:53,780
at the very, very most distal end of that blood supply is where you're going to get signs of ischemia.

136
00:09:53,780 --> 00:09:58,780
And it happens to be that the splenic flexure is the very last thing fed by the SMA.

137
00:09:58,780 --> 00:10:01,780
And so that's where the damage would tend to occur.

138
00:10:01,780 --> 00:10:06,780
Now, the way that this is going to present is with postprandial pain and weight loss.

139
00:10:06,780 --> 00:10:11,780
Now, the idea is that when the patient eats, they increase the energy requirements of the gut.

140
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:17,780
And so when you increase the energy requirements, that's the time that you're going to notice that the blood supply is not appropriate.

141
00:10:17,780 --> 00:10:19,780
And so they get postprandial pain.

142
00:10:19,780 --> 00:10:23,780
It's sort of like the way that a patient gets angina when they exercise or walk upstairs.

143
00:10:23,780 --> 00:10:29,780
You know, if they've got decreased blood flow going to the heart and then they exercise, then that's when they get the chest pain.

144
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:35,780
So in the same way, if you've got decreased blood flow going to the colon and then the patient exercises or eats,

145
00:10:35,780 --> 00:10:39,780
because that's the exercising of the colon, they're going to have postprandial pain.

146
00:10:39,780 --> 00:10:44,780
And at the same time, they lose weight because they get this pain and so they don't eat as much.

147
00:10:44,780 --> 00:10:50,780
If there is complete loss or severe loss of the blood supply, that would result in infarction.

148
00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:53,780
And that would again present with bloody diarrhea and pain.

149
00:10:53,780 --> 00:11:04,780
So ischemia would simply give you postprandial pain, whereas a continued lack of oxygen or severe lack of oxygen would give you infarction resulting in bloody diarrhea.

150
00:11:04,780 --> 00:11:15,780
Irritable bowel syndrome is a relapsing abdominal pain with bloating flatulence and a change in bowel habits, so the patients can have diarrhea or constipation.

151
00:11:15,780 --> 00:11:17,780
It improves with defecation.

152
00:11:17,780 --> 00:11:20,780
It's classically seen in middle-aged females.

153
00:11:20,780 --> 00:11:25,780
We don't have any identifiable pathologic changes in this disorder, so that if you did a biopsy, you wouldn't see much.

154
00:11:25,780 --> 00:11:32,780
However, we do know that it's related to a disturbed intestinal motility and that dietary fiber can improve the symptoms.

155
00:11:32,780 --> 00:11:38,780
The last discussion in this section concerns colonic polyps, and this is particularly high yield.

156
00:11:38,780 --> 00:11:42,780
Basically, with colonic polyps, you're dealing with a raised protrusion of the colonic mucosa.

157
00:11:42,780 --> 00:11:49,780
So that if this is the surface of the colon, we get this raised bump of the mucosa, and that's called a colonic polyp.

158
00:11:49,780 --> 00:11:53,780
There are two common types, and then there are some relatively rare types.

159
00:11:53,780 --> 00:11:55,780
We're going to focus on the two common types first.

160
00:11:55,780 --> 00:11:59,780
The most common type of polyp in the colon is a hyperplastic polyp.

161
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:01,780
This is due to hyperplasia of the glands.

162
00:12:01,780 --> 00:12:03,780
It's the most common type of polyp.

163
00:12:03,780 --> 00:12:10,780
It usually arises in the left colon, and it's benign with no malignant potential, and that's important.

164
00:12:10,780 --> 00:12:15,780
It is benign, and it doesn't have the ability were it to be left in the patient to become anything worse.

165
00:12:15,780 --> 00:12:20,780
Now, what I'd like to remind you of is that were you to look at this polyp on microscopy,

166
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:25,780
what you would see is that the surface of the polyp has this sort of sawtooth appearance,

167
00:12:25,780 --> 00:12:30,780
and that's called a serrated appearance, and that's the classic finding on microscopy.

168
00:12:30,780 --> 00:12:33,780
The second type of polyp is called an adenomatous polyp.

169
00:12:33,780 --> 00:12:37,780
This is a neoplastic proliferation of glands, again, second most common type.

170
00:12:37,780 --> 00:12:40,780
It is benign but super high yield.

171
00:12:40,780 --> 00:12:46,780
It has the ability were it to be left in a patient to become cancer, so it is pre-malignant,

172
00:12:46,780 --> 00:12:51,780
and it could become cancer via something called the adenoma carcinoma sequence.

173
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:53,780
Now, here's a picture of an adenomatous polyp.

174
00:12:53,780 --> 00:12:56,780
You can see this raised protrusion of colonic mucosa.

175
00:12:56,780 --> 00:12:59,780
Look at the normal glands here on the left bottom corner,

176
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:02,780
and then look at these glands which are much darker,

177
00:13:02,780 --> 00:13:08,780
and this represents the adenoma or the neoplastic proliferation of glands.

178
00:13:08,780 --> 00:13:14,780
Now, again, the key feature of these polyps is that they have the ability to undergo the adenoma carcinoma sequence,

179
00:13:14,780 --> 00:13:18,780
and this becomes super high yield for exam, so let's sort of draw a spectrum here.

180
00:13:18,780 --> 00:13:22,780
This is the surface of the colon, and in the normal colon, which I would draw here,

181
00:13:22,780 --> 00:13:25,780
there is no risk of developing a polyp.

182
00:13:25,780 --> 00:13:29,780
However, if the patient develops an APC gene mutation,

183
00:13:29,780 --> 00:13:34,780
APC is called, APC is adenominus polyposis coli gene.

184
00:13:34,780 --> 00:13:37,780
If the patient develops mutations in APC,

185
00:13:37,780 --> 00:13:41,780
then what can happen is that creates a risk for the development of a polyp.

186
00:13:41,780 --> 00:13:44,780
Now, of course, if you're at risk for development of a polyp,

187
00:13:44,780 --> 00:13:47,780
in the next step, you can get a KRAS mutation,

188
00:13:47,780 --> 00:13:51,780
and a KRAS mutation would eventually allow for the formation of that polyp,

189
00:13:51,780 --> 00:13:54,780
and eventually, that polyp can become carcinoma,

190
00:13:54,780 --> 00:13:58,780
and the way by which it can develop into carcinoma is that you can get P-firmary,

191
00:13:58,780 --> 00:14:04,780
you can get P-53 mutations, and you also need increased cyclooxygenase expression.

192
00:14:04,780 --> 00:14:09,780
These are two things that are important for development of carcinoma from an adenominus polyp.

193
00:14:09,780 --> 00:14:13,780
Now, this sequence is super high yield for examination purposes,

194
00:14:13,780 --> 00:14:15,780
and I'd like to highlight a couple points.

195
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:20,780
Number one, APC creates a risk for the development of polyps.

196
00:14:20,780 --> 00:14:25,780
APC is a tumor suppressor gene, so you have to knock out both copies to develop that risk.

197
00:14:25,780 --> 00:14:31,780
Now, normally, in patients in the age of 40, 50, 60, 70, as they develop a few polyps in their colon,

198
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:35,780
they knock out somatically both copies of APC.

199
00:14:35,780 --> 00:14:39,780
So there will be somatic mutation of both copies of APC,

200
00:14:39,780 --> 00:14:44,780
and when you knock out both copies of APC, that increases the risk to eventually develop a polyp.

201
00:14:44,780 --> 00:14:51,780
It is also possible to get a germline mutation of APC so that you knock out one copy because you received a bad copy from a parent,

202
00:14:51,780 --> 00:14:57,780
and that's called familial adenominus polyposis syndrome, and we'll talk about that in just a few minutes.

203
00:14:57,780 --> 00:15:01,780
So this is high yield, and it's also important to note that this is on chromosome 5.

204
00:15:01,780 --> 00:15:03,780
Those are all the high yields concerning APC.

205
00:15:03,780 --> 00:15:09,780
Tumor suppressor gene, when it's knocked out, you get the risk of developing a polyp.

206
00:15:09,780 --> 00:15:16,780
It can be knocked out creating a couple polyps in a normal patient, but in patients with familial adenominus polyposis,

207
00:15:16,780 --> 00:15:23,780
the gene is knocked out from a germline mutation, which then allows for the formation of thousands of polyps,

208
00:15:23,780 --> 00:15:28,780
as you're well aware of, which is a characteristic of familial adenominus polyposis.

209
00:15:28,780 --> 00:15:33,780
Okay, KRAS is the next mutation, and this allows for the formation of the polyp.

210
00:15:33,780 --> 00:15:37,780
And P53, super high yield, allows for the progression to carcinoma.

211
00:15:37,780 --> 00:15:43,780
Now notice that you also need increased cyclooxygenase, and one very high yield association here,

212
00:15:43,780 --> 00:15:47,780
shows up on exams often, is that aspirin protects against this sequence.

213
00:15:47,780 --> 00:15:52,780
So that patients who take aspirin, they will decrease the level of cyclooxygenase production,

214
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:57,780
and therefore they will protect against the adenoma carcinoma sequence, and that's very high yield.

215
00:15:57,780 --> 00:16:04,780
Now of course, because adenominus polyps are relatively common, and because they have the ability to become cancer,

216
00:16:04,780 --> 00:16:10,780
we want to screen for those polyps, and we want to get rid of them before they actually can develop into cancer.

217
00:16:10,780 --> 00:16:15,780
So the way by which we screen is colonoscopy, where we can go in and look for the polyps,

218
00:16:15,780 --> 00:16:19,780
and by testing for fecal occult blood, because sometimes these polyps can bleed.

219
00:16:19,780 --> 00:16:22,780
Again, the polyps are usually clinically silent, but they can bleed.

220
00:16:22,780 --> 00:16:28,780
Now, what's the goal? The goal is to remove that adenomatous polyp before it progresses to carcinoma.

221
00:16:28,780 --> 00:16:31,780
One of the best ways to screen for these polyps is colonoscopy,

222
00:16:31,780 --> 00:16:34,780
and what they do in colonoscopy is they take out all the polyps.

223
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:37,780
Now usually a patient at the age of 50 or 60 might have one or two polyps,

224
00:16:37,780 --> 00:16:41,780
they'll remove those polyps, and they'll examine them microscopically.

225
00:16:41,780 --> 00:16:44,780
The reason you have to take out all the polyps is because when you do colonoscopy,

226
00:16:44,780 --> 00:16:50,780
you can't tell the difference between a hyperplastic polyp and an adenomatous polyp.

227
00:16:50,780 --> 00:16:53,780
The only thing you see is a polyp, and so you take a piece,

228
00:16:53,780 --> 00:16:56,780
and under the microscopy, we'll be able to make that distinction.

229
00:16:56,780 --> 00:16:59,780
So all the polyps are removed and examined microscopically.

230
00:16:59,780 --> 00:17:02,780
Now, one of the important things that they'd like you to know for examination

231
00:17:02,780 --> 00:17:06,780
is that if you have an adenoma, let's say that there's a colonoscopy,

232
00:17:06,780 --> 00:17:09,780
and an adenoma is removed, the question you could ask is,

233
00:17:09,780 --> 00:17:13,780
what was the risk of that particular adenoma to become cancer?

234
00:17:13,780 --> 00:17:15,780
Now, it's out of the patient, so it wouldn't become cancer,

235
00:17:15,780 --> 00:17:19,780
but what was the theoretical risk for that adenoma to become cancer?

236
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:23,780
And there are three key risk factors that tell you that there was an increased theoretical risk.

237
00:17:23,780 --> 00:17:26,780
Number one, size greater than two centimeters.

238
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:28,780
If you have a polyp greater than two centimeters,

239
00:17:28,780 --> 00:17:31,780
that would suggest an increased theoretical risk.

240
00:17:31,780 --> 00:17:34,780
Also, if there's sessile growth, what does that mean?

241
00:17:34,780 --> 00:17:36,780
When you look at polyps, polyps can kind of grow in two ways.

242
00:17:36,780 --> 00:17:38,780
The first way, as I'm illustrating here,

243
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:41,780
is a pedunculated polyp where it grows off a stalk.

244
00:17:41,780 --> 00:17:45,780
And the other way would be for the polyp to go flat along the wall.

245
00:17:45,780 --> 00:17:48,780
So this pattern is called the sessile pattern,

246
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:51,780
and this pattern is called the pedunculated pattern.

247
00:17:51,780 --> 00:18:00,780
And the sessile pattern theoretically has the more risk to develop carcinoma.

248
00:18:00,780 --> 00:18:02,780
And then the final is the histology.

249
00:18:02,780 --> 00:18:04,780
When you look at the histology of a polyp,

250
00:18:04,780 --> 00:18:07,780
the polyp can have a bunch of these tubular spaces,

251
00:18:07,780 --> 00:18:10,780
in which case it's called a tubular adenoma,

252
00:18:10,780 --> 00:18:15,780
or the polyp can have a bunch of long villus-like projections coming from it,

253
00:18:15,780 --> 00:18:18,780
in which case it's called a villus adenoma.

254
00:18:18,780 --> 00:18:22,780
And so the villus histology is theoretically more worrisome

255
00:18:22,780 --> 00:18:26,780
for the development of, for the potential development of carcinoma.

256
00:18:26,780 --> 00:18:32,780
And there's that funny mnemonic that the villus histology is the villain in this particular discussion.

257
00:18:32,780 --> 00:18:36,780
Now familial adenomatous polyposis is an autosomal dominant disorder

258
00:18:36,780 --> 00:18:38,780
in which you knock out the APC gene.

259
00:18:38,780 --> 00:18:40,780
If the APC gene gets knocked out,

260
00:18:40,780 --> 00:18:46,780
then of course the patient now has a colonic mucosa that's at massive risk for development of polyps,

261
00:18:46,780 --> 00:18:50,780
and over time you'll get progression to KRAS mutations,

262
00:18:50,780 --> 00:18:56,780
and that'll result in the production of hundreds to thousands of adenomatous colonic polyps.

263
00:18:56,780 --> 00:19:00,780
So this is the picture of familial adenomatous polyposis,

264
00:19:00,780 --> 00:19:04,780
and you can see this whole surface of the colon is basically loaded with polyps.

265
00:19:04,780 --> 00:19:08,780
Now in these patients you have to remove the colon and the rectum prophylactically,

266
00:19:08,780 --> 00:19:10,780
and the reason is that once they get these polyps,

267
00:19:10,780 --> 00:19:17,780
the possibility, the random possibility of one of these polyps progressing to get a P53 mutation

268
00:19:17,780 --> 00:19:21,780
and increased cyclooxygenase, that is very likely.

269
00:19:21,780 --> 00:19:25,780
And so basically because there's a very, very high risk of development of cancer,

270
00:19:25,780 --> 00:19:27,780
the colon and the rectum are removed.

271
00:19:27,780 --> 00:19:29,780
And in fact, if you don't remove the colon and the rectum,

272
00:19:29,780 --> 00:19:34,780
the vast majority of these patients will actually develop carcinoma by the age of 40.

273
00:19:34,780 --> 00:19:36,780
Now patients who have familial adenomatous polyposis

274
00:19:36,780 --> 00:19:38,780
can sometimes have some associations with it,

275
00:19:38,780 --> 00:19:40,780
and that would change the name of the disorder.

276
00:19:40,780 --> 00:19:44,780
And so for example, if the patient has familial adenomatous polyposis

277
00:19:44,780 --> 00:19:47,780
and also has fibromatosis and osteomas,

278
00:19:47,780 --> 00:19:49,780
then that would be called Gardner syndrome.

279
00:19:49,780 --> 00:19:53,780
Fibromatosis is a non-neoplastic proliferation of fibroblasts,

280
00:19:53,780 --> 00:19:55,780
usually arises in the retroperitoneum,

281
00:19:55,780 --> 00:19:58,780
and it's destructive, locally destructive.

282
00:19:58,780 --> 00:20:01,780
And then an osteoma is a benign tumor of bone,

283
00:20:01,780 --> 00:20:02,780
usually arises in the skull.

284
00:20:02,780 --> 00:20:05,780
So the presence of FAP, familial adenomatous polyposis,

285
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:08,780
familial adenomatous polyposis with fibromatosis and osteomas,

286
00:20:08,780 --> 00:20:10,780
that's called Gardner syndrome.

287
00:20:10,780 --> 00:20:14,780
Turcotte syndrome is where you have FAP with CNS tumors,

288
00:20:14,780 --> 00:20:17,780
in particular medulloblastoma and glial tumors.

289
00:20:17,780 --> 00:20:20,780
Now there are some rare types of polyps that can also arise,

290
00:20:20,780 --> 00:20:23,780
and I promised to discuss these at the very end of this section.

291
00:20:23,780 --> 00:20:25,780
The first is a juvenile polyp.

292
00:20:25,780 --> 00:20:26,780
This is a hemartoma.

293
00:20:26,780 --> 00:20:31,780
Remember that a hemartoma is a tumor that's comprised of the tissue

294
00:20:31,780 --> 00:20:34,780
that would normally be present, however it's disorganized.

295
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:37,780
It's a sporadic hemartoma, so therefore it's benign,

296
00:20:37,780 --> 00:20:40,780
that arises in children less than the age of five.

297
00:20:40,780 --> 00:20:43,780
Usually it presents as a solitary rectal polyp,

298
00:20:43,780 --> 00:20:45,780
and that can prolapse and bleed.

299
00:20:45,780 --> 00:20:47,780
So that's called a juvenile polyp.

300
00:20:47,780 --> 00:20:49,780
Now it's possible to have multiple juvenile polyps,

301
00:20:49,780 --> 00:20:52,780
due to some sort of polyposis syndrome.

302
00:20:52,780 --> 00:20:54,780
In that particular case, you get a whole bunch of juvenile polyps

303
00:20:54,780 --> 00:20:56,780
in the stomach and the colon,

304
00:20:56,780 --> 00:20:58,780
and because you've got so many juvenile polyps,

305
00:20:58,780 --> 00:21:01,780
it does increase the risk of progression to carcinoma.

306
00:21:01,780 --> 00:21:04,780
So only with large number of juvenile polyps

307
00:21:04,780 --> 00:21:07,780
would you worry about a risk for progression to carcinoma.

308
00:21:07,780 --> 00:21:10,780
Poutierger syndrome is where you get hemartomatous polyps

309
00:21:10,780 --> 00:21:11,780
throughout the GI tract,

310
00:21:11,780 --> 00:21:15,780
but it's also associated with mucocutaneous hyperpigmentation

311
00:21:15,780 --> 00:21:18,780
on the lips, the oral mucosa, and the genital skin.

312
00:21:18,780 --> 00:21:20,780
This is an autosomal dominant disorder.

313
00:21:20,780 --> 00:21:23,780
There's an increased risk in these patients for colorectal,

314
00:21:23,780 --> 00:21:27,780
breast, and gynecologic malignancies.

315
00:21:27,780 --> 00:21:29,780
And so that's Poutier-Jager syndrome.

316
00:21:29,780 --> 00:21:30,780
That closes off our section on colon,

317
00:21:30,780 --> 00:21:33,780
and the last section in the chapter is colorectal carcinoma.

